From 4981c712f43d53eed3a5f386001e84f79ceae113 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: utzcoz <utzcoz@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:35:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] vr: show the WebXR browser overlay on Linux

The in-headset browser overlay (permission prompts and capture
indicators) was turned off on Linux: OpenXrDevice dropped the
ImmersiveOverlay remote, so the browser never created a VrUiHost.
Turn it on:

- Add GraphicsDelegateLinux, which draws the overlay like
  GraphicsDelegateWin, into a GPU-only SharedImage: Ozone/X11 has no
  native buffers to share by default. It clears the image every frame,
  as WebXR frames without overlay elements draw nothing, and it
  allocates the gles2_lib thread-local key that the VR UI's GL calls
  use.
- The Vulkan binding copies each new overlay frame into an image of
  its own, exported like the LINEAR intermediate images, and
  alpha-blends each eye's part of it into the projection layer it
  shows with a small graphics pipeline. While the overlay hides
  WebXR, and in the base layer above the page's own layers, it is
  drawn over a cleared image instead.
- SendFrameData() keeps the shared images of a frame it suppresses.
  A frame started just before the overlay hid WebXR had them, and
  Blink DCHECKs that every frame with an id does.

Bug: 506004811
Change-Id: Id815250e4c1b40132e9fb4e0fa43c42df7db571f
